The Shrewd Dude wrote:
> The idea is to make items slightly more fun, and a tad bit more
> difficult to identify.

A worthy quest.

> Wand of Harm Self
> -does not ask for a direction: is always targeted towards yourself.
> -when broken, the magical explosion deals d20, only towards yourself.
> -engraving with the wand causes it to zap.
> -is never generated blessed.
> blessed
> Confuses or stuns you.
> ("Your mind spins wildly!")
> uncursed
> Adds the effects of hallucination ("Far out, dude!"), wands of
> striking, magic missile, fire, cold, and lightning to the list of
> possible effects. Additionally, abuses your stats.
>
> cursed
> Adds the effects of polymorph, bad sex, bad spellbooks, and cursed
> potions to the list of possible effects. Additionally, has the effect
> of a cursed potion of gain ability.

It is worth noting that wands do not have unique effects for blessed or
cursed items.  They are possibly the item category in the game it is
least useful to bless or uncurse.  The only effect a cursed wand
carries is a small chance of exploding, and the explosion doesn't even
have a wand-specific result.

A wand of harm self, from a gameplay stance, isn't really bad but it
makes more sense for it to be a different type of item perhaps.  Here's
how I consider the what the different magic item types in Nethack imply
about item design:

Potions: Should affect the player only when quaffed.  Potions imply a
kind of chemical or biological process produces the results, and so
they are a very "logical" item type.  Keep that in mind when coming up
with secondary uses, like vapor and dipping effects, and potion mixing.
 They also produce special effects depending on cursed or blessed
state, making it difficult to design new potion types, but they are
potentially a very interesting item class.

Scrolls: Can affect most anything when read, but when the target is
yourself it's best used for things that are not logical with potions.
The DevTeam does't always follow this rule, though.  Scrolls imply a
magical process, and have special effects (oddly consistent ones) when
read while confused, as well as when blessed or cursed.  They also can
be written with a magic marker.  The only other special scroll effect
is that scrolls of scare monster work as an Elbereth engraving (or more
accurately vice-versa) when on the ground.  (There is a patch floating
around, however, that gives them throw effects and allows monsters to
throw them too.)

Wands: Again there are exceptions, but wands are best used for
directional effects (especially rays), and general cases where a magic
item needs to have multiple uses, but ultimately a limited number of
them (they can be recharged of course).  Wands can be zapped, broken or
engraved with.  They don't have special blessed or cursed effects.

Rings: They can be worn or (in certain cases) eaten.  (Other magic
items can be eaten too, but they don't provide special effects like
rings and amulets do.)  If it's blessed there's no effect, cursed rings
are prevented from being removed until the curse is lifted but
otherwise do not change it in any way.  They usually increase food
consumption, generally in proportion to the usefulness of the ring,
although rings of regeneration increase food consumption also for
historical reasons.

Amulets: They can be worn or eaten, and like rings, the only effect of
their being cursed is that they cannot be removed.  Amulet
identification is made a bit trickier because of the amulet of
strangulation, an item that casts a pall over the whole class when it
comes to identifying through experimentation.


> Wand of Darkness
> -when engraving, zaps the wand.
> blessed
> The area around you is darkened.
> uncursed
> You are blinded.
> cursed
> You are blinded, and suffer from malignant aura.

Hm, interesting.  The bless/curse effects though have to go to fit in
with the rest of Nethack's current b/u/c scheme.

> Wand of Undeath
> -no message when engraving
> -when broken, acts as a wand of undead turning
> -beams and touches of death will cause the wand to explode
> You are polymorphed into the appropriate type of zombie. If you are
> currently a living creature without an appropriate type of zombie, you
> are reverted to your original form and then zombified. If you are
> already undead, no effect.

Not logical: why undead turning effect when broken?  Also note that
zombies are the same size of their base forms so no items will be
destroyed by such a zap, and unless the player is wearing an amulet of
unchanging (which logically should prevent the change in the first
place), players are actually *safer*, generally, when polymorphed than
not, since when the new form runs out of hit points he'll revert to
normal, and healed.


> Wand of Destroy Armor
> -zaps at self when engraving
> -when broken, deals n15 damage to non-magic resistant creatures in
> vicinity; destroys armor
> Fires a beam of destroy armor; destroys a random piece of armor.
> If cursed, has a chance of firing at yourself.

Basically, this is a limited-application disintegration beam, then?
One without a chance of instakilling?  That could be interesting.

How would the player protect himself from it though?  Is it a ray
effect?  I consider that the game needs more things that can happen in
the late game that could break through an ascension kit's all-purpose
utility, but it could royally suck for a player in the early game.
Black Dragons, at least, are fairly obvious dangers, but a monster's
wand of destroy armor doesn't make itself known until it is zapped.

On the whole, however, I kind of like the idea.


> Ring of Sliming
> -begins the sliming process.
> -if sliming is stopped, ring is destroyed.
> -auto-identifies.

No, way too dangerous.  At least prayer gets you out of an amulet of
strangulation: it doesn't help currently (to my knowledge) with
sliming.


> Ring of Paralysis
> -you are frozen to the ground for n300 turns.
> -the ring dissolves after the effect takes place.
> -auto-identifies.

Rings are already an item class it is very difficult to discover
through experimentation due to the large number of subtle ring effects.
 This would add an unprecedented element of danger to it that I'm not
too fond of.


> Ring of Magic Portal
> -you are branch-ported; randomly if not blessed.
> -ring is used up when used up.

No, doesn't fit the use profile of rings.


> Anti-Personnel Ring
> -you suffer from an exploded land mine.
> -ring is used up when used up.

See above.


> Amulet of Anchoring
> -acts like a loadstone if cursed
> -otherwise, prevents you from teleporting

Hm, interesting.  A "cure" for teleportitis, but uses up the amulet
slot.  Not exciting, but interesting still.


> Amulet versus Oxidation
> -your items will not rust further
> -you gain rust resistance

The traditional name for this effect (in Rogue) is "maintain armor,"
and it's usually a ring.  It makes more sense as a ring, too, since the
existence of permanent rustproofing makes it a bit useless anywhere
except the early game (where Rs don't even show up).  (That was what
was wrong with Rogue's ring as well in versions of the game that have
protect armor scrolls.)


> Amulet versus Water
> -drys up moats, pools of water, rust traps, and fountains adjacent to
> you
> -has a chance of being used up each time this occurs
> -clerics will not attack you with geysers

A good idea, but not as an amulet.


> Amulet versus Fire
> -your items will not burn further
> -you gain fire resistance

We already have a ring with this effect, and it barely gets used.  I'd
move the no-item-burning to the ring and to without the amulet.


> Amulet versus Earth
> -you take d2 damage every time you step on solid ground
> -provides water walking
> -this is considered a major problem

Hm, not too interested in this.  Random magic items that mostly just
cause damage are slightly bad (but in abundance in Nethack anyway), and
there's already an amulet that helps out with water.


> Amulet versus Air
> -see; amulet of strangulation; amulet of magical breathing

Not good, sorry.

